WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
(time code beeps)
(projector clacks)

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.810
- To my right is "Bounty Law" series lead

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.290
and Jake Cahill himself, Rick Dalton,

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.566
and to my left is Rick's
stunt double Cliff Booth.

00:00:07.566 --> 00:00:10.399
(energetic music)

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.610
So Rick, explain to the audience

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.230
exactly what it is a stunt double does.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.288
- Actors are required to do
a lot of dangerous stuff.

00:00:19.288 --> 00:00:20.121
(gunshot cracks)

00:00:20.121 --> 00:00:20.954
(fist thuds)

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:22.330
(gunshots crack)
(men yelling)

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.490
Cliff here is meant to
help carry the load.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.210
- Is that how you
describe your job, Cliff?

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.260
- What? Carrying his load?

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.654
Yeah, it's about right.

00:00:30.654 --> 00:00:35.654
(interviewer laughing)
(frantic music)

00:00:43.123 --> 00:00:47.020
♪ La-la-la-la la la la la ♪

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.020
- My hands are registered
as lethal weapons.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.230
We get into a fight, I
accidentally kill you,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.063
I go to jail.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.420
- Anybody accidentally
kills anybody in a fight,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:58.253
they go to jail.

00:00:58.253 --> 00:00:59.440
It's called manslaughter.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.273
(boisterous music)

00:01:00.273 --> 00:01:03.007
(fists thudding)
(men grunting)

00:01:03.007 --> 00:01:03.840
♪ Won't you, won't you, won't you ♪

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.885
♪ Make a little money ♪

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:07.910
♪ Drink a little wine ♪

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.868
♪ Bring a little lovin ♪

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:12.095
♪ And I feel fine ♪

00:01:12.095 --> 00:01:16.010
♪ Come in here and love with me ♪

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.158
- [Director] And cut.

00:01:17.158 --> 00:01:18.783
(bell dings)

00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:22.773
- That was the best acting I've
ever seen in my whole life.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.333
- Like you.

00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:27.900
(Leonardo sobs)

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.582
Rick fuckin' Dalton.

00:01:29.582 --> 00:01:32.760
(gun clicks)
(frantic music)

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.760
(Leonardo sighs)
(projector clacking)

00:01:48.763 --> 00:01:49.760
- Hello, everyone, and welcome

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.260
to the Academy Visiting
Artists Film Series,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.790
FilmAid edition for casting directors.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.950
I'm Bernie Telsey, casting director,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.170
and I'm here today with my good friend,

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.273
fellow casting director Vickie Thomas.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.890
How are you today, Vickie?

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.410
- Hi, there. How are you, Bernie?

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.243
- I'm good.

00:02:07.243 --> 00:02:09.470
I feel like I wish I wasn't in
New York so far away from you

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:10.755
so we could do it in-person.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:12.666
- I know. Well, you know.

00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:14.201
I know how hard it is.
- How are you?

00:02:14.201 --> 00:02:15.107
I'm good. I'm good.

00:02:15.107 --> 00:02:18.080
I'm looking forward to a
very long, long weekend, so-

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.460
- Yes, exactly. Yes, exactly.
- (indistinct), you know?

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.840
- Well, we're here today to talk about

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.130
what it is that you and I
do every day, (chuckles)

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.900
and for all these wonderful film students,

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.450
so I'd love to start with just saying

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.510
how did you get into casting?

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.360
And then, how did you transition

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.163
into having your own business?

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.950
- Well, I didn't get into
casting via the usual route.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.780
I never worked for anyone.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.770
It was really just me

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.660
working with a fellow
film student at UCLA,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.110
and they asked me

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.090
to cast their student thesis film,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.567
and I went, "Okay."

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.440
We would always work
on each other's films,

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.730
whether we were making lunch for the crew

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.040
or holding a boom mic or...

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.900
Everybody just kinda pitched
in and helped each other,

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:07.733
and I think someone said,

00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:08.893
"Well, maybe Vickie should cast that,"

00:03:08.893 --> 00:03:11.500
and, okay, they asked me,
and I said, "Okay, sure,"

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.870
and that student thesis film

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.160
evolved over the course of a year

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.670
and became a low-budget independent
movie called "Repo Man,"

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.260
and they just kept me on

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.343
when the film actually became
like a legitimate movie,

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.910
and yeah, just sort of got thrown

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.230
into the deep end of the pool

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.440
and tried to learn

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.590
what it meant to be a casting director

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.310
or how you cast a film,

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.720
so I didn't have the privilege of really,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.167
of working for someone

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:50.130
and sort of being taught the ropes

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.175
and coming up through the ranks,

00:03:53.175 --> 00:03:56.250
which I would've really enjoyed.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.810
I just kinda, I got thrown in

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.610
and I just had to learn how to swim.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.520
- And then, do you
remember this sort of time

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:03.877
when you woke up one day and said,

00:04:03.877 --> 00:04:06.307
"Oh, I'm gonna do this for a living

00:04:06.307 --> 00:04:09.040
"and I'm gonna do this for my profession"?

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.390
- Yeah, it's funny 'cause I did that,

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.796
and I went, "Oh, that's
just sort of a one-off,"

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:13.937
and then I think I went

00:04:13.937 --> 00:04:16.110
and worked a couple of
other jobs or whatever,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.530
and then I came back,

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.020
and the same producer of the film

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.310
asked me to help him out in another movie,

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.750
and then I got a call that didn't happen.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.730
Then I got a call from
Ed Pressman's office,

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.369
someone who actually
didn't know me, (laughs)

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:32.830
so a friend who was giving me a job,

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.630
and they wanted to hire me to
be their casting consultant.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:36.463
I went, "Okay,"

00:04:36.463 --> 00:04:38.253
and to cast a movie with David Byrne.

00:04:39.491 --> 00:04:41.764
I went, "Oh, okay, sure,"

00:04:41.764 --> 00:04:43.700
and I went in my little meeting

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.620
and made my little notes and
had my little suggestions

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.817
and then, yeah, I think after that I went,

00:04:49.817 --> 00:04:54.367
"Oh, I guess I could put
something in that occupation slot

00:04:54.367 --> 00:04:57.420
"on whatever piece of paper you fill out,"

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.977
and it was right around then I went,

00:04:58.977 --> 00:05:00.727
"Oh, I guess casting director."

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.040
- Right. That's great.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.330
What do you love about casting?

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:09.260
And what are some of
your guiding principles

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.853
when you start casting a film?

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:12.773
- I think I love,

00:05:12.773 --> 00:05:13.930
I think, like a lot of casting directors,

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:15.290
I love working with the actors,

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.717
just getting in a room, and it's like,

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.757
"Let's run the scene

00:05:19.757 --> 00:05:23.340
"and let's try something different."

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.190
I think just the actual
process of auditioning actors,

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:31.670
which has been a little
hampered by, obviously,

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:34.660
the COVID times we're living in,

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.657
but that's really the best for me,

00:05:36.657 --> 00:05:40.113
and just seeing someone kind of flower,

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.440
see a performance sort of come
alive in an audition room,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.770
and look, I guess, like
all casting directors,

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.140
you obviously wanna cast the best,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.740
the perfect person for the part,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.440
the best actor you can,

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.730
and hopefully, make some
sort of surprising choice

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:57.563
along the way,

00:05:57.563 --> 00:06:00.350
and hopefully, maybe
break someone, you know?

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:01.930
- Yeah, that's the best.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.150
- Yeah, that's really
the exciting part of it,

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.283
so yeah, I mean, do you
have any principles?

00:06:08.283 --> 00:06:12.120
I mean, just in terms of what do you...

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:12.953
- I don't know.

00:06:12.953 --> 00:06:15.640
One of my biggest principles
is just being nice

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.690
and being available

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:21.080
to all of the different kinds
of people that we work with,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.353
whether it be the actor or director.

00:06:22.353 --> 00:06:25.610
There's such a personality business,

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:29.300
and learning how to be
that host, so to speak,

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.810
of how to engage with each person,

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.100
whether we agree or don't agree.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.470
- Yeah, and everybody's got a different...

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.910
Yeah, so there are a lot
of different personalities,

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:44.050
and yes, it's like surfing.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.127
It's sort of like

00:06:46.276 --> 00:06:50.260
just 'cause you, yeah,
there's never one experience

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:52.240
or one type of personality or one...

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:55.760
It's just they're all different,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.120
so you kinda have to be able to adjust.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.730
- Can you share a little
bit about it's day one,

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.100
and you're about to
work on this new movie,

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.620
and you've read it.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.520
What are those early
first conversations like

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:09.783
with you and a director?

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.740
- Well, usually, I like
to have conversations

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:18.080
where it's not just about the casting.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.280
It's sort of the overall
feel of the movie,

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.053
the overall story,

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.140
just sorta what we're trying to achieve

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:28.853
and how casting can help that,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:31.800
so I think it helps to be brought along,

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.860
as a casting director, into
the filmmaking process,

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.250
and just as a fellow filmmaker

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.280
'cause we're all, hopefully,
trying to go for the same goal,

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.590
and yeah, I just think a sense

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.263
of the overall picture,

00:07:50.818 --> 00:07:53.640
and yeah, then you, obviously
you get into the casting,

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.580
you get into characters, you get into...

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:58.257
I like conversations
where they're sort of,

00:07:58.257 --> 00:08:00.647
"Well, who would this character
have been in the '50s?

00:08:00.647 --> 00:08:03.217
"What actor would've played this role

00:08:03.217 --> 00:08:04.837
"if we were casting this in the '50s

00:08:04.837 --> 00:08:06.177
"or the '60s"-
- Yeah, yeah, I like that too.

00:08:06.177 --> 00:08:08.980
- like that 'cause that actually kind of,

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:11.710
that, surprisingly, gives
me a real good sense

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.623
of, "Oh, it's, okay, well, it's..."

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:16.760
- Well, it's a nice way to start

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.240
'cause it takes you out
of talking about someone

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.073
in the moment.

00:08:21.073 --> 00:08:21.906
You know what I mean?

00:08:21.906 --> 00:08:22.739
It's like-
- Yes.

00:08:23.634 --> 00:08:24.570
- Kind of-
- Well, it kind of allows you

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:25.480
to sort of...

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.210
It helps in free associating,

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.000
helps in kind of getting
out of a sort of a

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:35.590
such a maybe a closed off

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.860
sort of mentality

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:41.640
about the current actors
of today or whatever,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.650
so it's just sort of
freeing, a little bit,

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.007
- you know?
- Yeah.

00:08:45.007 --> 00:08:45.880
Well, you-
- And it's fun.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:48.150
Yeah, you bring up a good point

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:49.960
because so much of the relationship

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.620
between a casting
director and the director

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:54.790
is the collaboration
and the give-and-take,

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:56.000
as you were saying,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.030
but what are some of the,

00:08:58.030 --> 00:08:59.490
what do you do

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.220
when you do come up
against that conversation

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:04.110
about a present actor

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.220
that you, particularly,
might be passionate about,

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:10.450
and the director, he or she, is not.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:14.850
How do you help change
a mind, potentially,

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.983
or what do you do when you come
across that stumbling block?

00:09:17.983 --> 00:09:21.417
- You know, you sorta maybe plant a seed.

00:09:21.417 --> 00:09:23.082
You put a picture over there

00:09:23.082 --> 00:09:27.100
and move it along, come
back in a few days,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.977
and go, "Huh, you know?

00:09:28.977 --> 00:09:30.047
"Maybe the..."

00:09:30.047 --> 00:09:31.097
"No, no, no, no. We don't want that."

00:09:31.097 --> 00:09:33.427
"Okay, okay. Well, let's
just go on and keep moving.

00:09:33.427 --> 00:09:35.360
"We'll keep reading people,"

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.197
and this actually just happened recently.

00:09:38.197 --> 00:09:39.977
"No, definitely not.
They're definitely wrong."

00:09:39.977 --> 00:09:41.783
"Okay. Well, let's just keep going.

00:09:42.677 --> 00:09:43.647
"Let's keep reading people

00:09:43.647 --> 00:09:45.707
"and try to find what
you're really looking for

00:09:45.707 --> 00:09:47.193
"or that fits your..."

00:09:48.506 --> 00:09:50.187
You keep going, and
you go, "You know what?

00:09:50.187 --> 00:09:52.567
"That guy, that actor that... (laughs)

00:09:52.567 --> 00:09:54.580
"Well, you wanna look at him again?"

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:57.593
So you just kinda plant a
seed, sorta bring it back,

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:03.210
and usually, through
the process of casting,

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.870
something may open up
for the director as well

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:09.030
if you sort of are going
for something so specific

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:12.960
that it's sort of you're
not quite finding it,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.760
and then you might find
actually what you're looking for

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:20.523
with an actor that you didn't
expect to get that from.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.140
Actually, what you're talking
about for this character,

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.470
this actor actually brings to you

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:28.660
in a slightly different way,

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:33.660
but that might work just
as well, if not better.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.093
- Right, right.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:41.893
Do you find the conversations
that are happening today,

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.850
based on the recent
racial justice movement

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:48.950
that we've all been part
of in the last two years,

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.330
have those conversations been easier,

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.270
harder to have with creative team members

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:56.730
about inclusion and
belonging and diversity

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.523
as far as where it relates to actors?

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.860
- Well, easier and harder

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.550
because, look, yes,

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.880
let's find roles for actors of all races.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.680
Let's reflect a world as it exists,

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:15.300
and if that happens to be
a multiracial world, great,

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.370
but also, if it's like,

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.233
if we're doing a film about the Amish,

00:11:19.233 --> 00:11:23.070
I mean, I don't really wanna
shoehorn a actor of color

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.580
into necessarily a film about the Amish,

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.360
and you have to be careful.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:31.600
Sometimes I don't wanna just...

00:11:33.186 --> 00:11:35.660
I want the best actor for the part,

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.833
and I don't want tokenism,

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:41.893
so how do you...

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.640
It's just, it can get a little tricky,

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:46.777
the conversations where it's like,

00:11:46.777 --> 00:11:47.610
"Well, it has to be
this or has to be that."

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:49.393
It starts to look like a,

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.220
like sort of a Benetton ad

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:53.860
or something that's being put on

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:56.810
not in a natural, organic way,

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:59.627
so that's sometimes, those
are tricky conversations

00:11:59.627 --> 00:12:01.350
'cause I think I find myself

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:05.960
sometimes as the only
black woman in the room

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:09.370
arguing for the white actor
to play that particular part.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:11.310
- Right. Sure, sure.
(Vickie laughs)

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.747
- It just, well, I just think,

00:12:12.747 --> 00:12:16.237
"Well, that, it just seems
to fit that role better

00:12:16.237 --> 00:12:18.017
"and achieve what you're trying to achieve

00:12:18.017 --> 00:12:21.700
"if that character is a white actor."

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:22.533
You know what I'm saying?

00:12:22.533 --> 00:12:25.020
I know we're all trying to be inclusive,

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:26.880
but just wanna make sure

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.010
it's not getting in the way
of what you're trying to do

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:31.970
with the film or the television project.

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:32.840
Does that make sense?

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:37.263
- Yeah, no, no, I feel like,
for me, it's been a little,

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.030
it's been enlightening, and I don't wanna,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:42.240
easier isn't the right word,

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.860
but now I feel like on day one

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:48.820
the teams are wanting us to
lead those conversations.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:52.820
I mean, I know you and I have
been seeing diverse actors

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:54.450
since the day we started in casting

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:56.260
'cause why wouldn't you?

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:57.093
It was always better

00:12:57.093 --> 00:13:00.800
to play with a bigger crayon
box of actors than a smaller,

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:02.520
but then, the decision-making

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.850
is what always didn't
happen until the end,

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:07.580
and then it was too late
to have those conversations

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:09.340
about why can't it be?

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:13.090
And now I find it's like
the first day of school.

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:16.590
I walk in and, "Let's talk
about what this role is

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:17.617
"and what does it really mean?

00:13:17.617 --> 00:13:19.710
"And why can't it be or why can it be?"

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.210
- Yeah, well, I find that, I think,

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.900
these conversations aren't
being led by us anymore.

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:29.006
I think the conversations
are being led by the studios.

00:13:29.006 --> 00:13:31.880
They're being led by some producers.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:34.820
They're being led by
the network or what...

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.340
It's like there has definitely been,

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.770
in the last, I dunno,
Bernie, what do you think?

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:43.370
Three, four years?
- Yeah.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.654
- We don't have to encourage
anybody to... (laughs)

00:13:46.654 --> 00:13:47.760
- Right, right.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.970
We have to remind them.
- (indistinct) mandate.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.680
- Right. Now it's reminding, and...

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:53.513
- Yeah.

00:13:55.837 --> 00:13:58.790
- And then, let's talk
about that challenge.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:13:59.880
What do you do

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.570
when you're faced against the challenge

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:04.220
of the bankable actor

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.930
that will bring the financing
to an independent film

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:08.090
or a studio film

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:12.340
versus who might be
better suited creatively?

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:15.820
I mean, how do you handle
those conversations?

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.700
- Well, I mean, it's kind of a
reality we have to deal with.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:20.050
I mean, it's,

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:23.650
and I understand someone is
putting up a lot of money

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:25.523
for a film.

00:14:25.523 --> 00:14:29.100
They like to get at least some sense

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.540
that they're gonna get their money back

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:33.100
and maybe make some money,

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.760
so I understand that.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.150
I think all of that
isn't always guaranteed,

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:40.210
but it's just

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:45.340
I understand people wanting that comfort,

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.910
so you just try to

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.860
maybe balance the known,

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.210
give the, well, this
is the unknown version;

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.777
this is the known version,

00:14:54.777 --> 00:14:56.240
and if something happens,

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:58.730
maybe you get your lead in this role

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:00.710
and maybe you can slip
in someone less known

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:03.220
who's great in this other role,

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.610
but I mean it's just a reality

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.170
of the business that we work in.

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:09.980
On some projects, it's a lot more,

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:14.783
like, "No, we need that and we need..."

00:15:16.384 --> 00:15:17.360
It's not like a Chinese menu,

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.810
but it's just like they
want bankable people

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:21.680
in all these roles.

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:24.600
If it's a big franchise
movie, they want...

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:25.700
- Right. They want-

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:27.360
- That's what they want.
(Bernie speaks indistinctly)

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:29.850
Yeah, and it's you just have
to sort of deal with that,

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:30.683
and like I said,

00:15:30.683 --> 00:15:32.540
maybe try to slip in
something a little surprising

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:33.410
along the way

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:34.530
in some of the other parts,

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.370
but it's a reality we have to deal with.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:38.393
- Right. Right.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.140
You've worked on a bunch of films

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.020
by the same director or producer,

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.480
and can you talk a
little bit, specifically,

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:51.560
about what those collaborations
and relationships are like

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:52.393
and what they were like

00:15:52.393 --> 00:15:55.870
from the first time you
worked with he or she

00:15:55.870 --> 00:16:00.390
versus now, the fifth
time or, and what those,

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:01.650
and how your relationship

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:04.650
between the casting director
and director grows and changes.

00:16:05.485 --> 00:16:09.010
- I guess maybe you develop
a little more of a shorthand

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:11.853
as you work with people more and more,

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:17.670
and with Ed Zwick,

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.240
it's like he's my brother.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:20.280
It's like we argue a lot,

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.130
and so it's sort of

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.320
almost like becomes sort
of a familial relationship,

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:29.910
but in a great sort of joking way,

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.940
but we, I think, feel familiar
enough with each other

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.650
to get on each other's nerves

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:38.720
and be fine with the fact

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:40.100
that we're getting on each other's nerves

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:41.110
'cause it's gonna be okay,

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:44.763
and I'm not gonna be thrown
out of the room or something.

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:47.590
With someone like Quentin,

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:50.090
it's just we had a shorthand immediately

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:54.440
just 'cause we had similar
cultural references,

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:56.790
similar film references,

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:01.660
similar memories of
television shows we looked at,

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:05.040
and so we had, we got
along very, very quickly.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:05.873
- Right, right,

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:11.450
and is it different now working
with Quentin than it was?

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:14.020
- Just as more, just more...

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:15.890
We just laugh a lot.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.430
We laugh a lot more, and just...

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:23.520
It's more relaxed, more comfortable,

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:27.060
just more intimate, in a way,

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.780
but it was pretty close from the get-go,

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:33.000
and look,

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:35.710
sometimes you have relationships
with directors, Bernie,

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:36.880
and you work with them,

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.870
and sometimes people feel like

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:44.320
they wanna just try something
different or just...

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:47.380
And you kinda, you have to watch

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:50.010
that you don't sort of take it personally

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.180
or get hurt by it.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:55.080
Sometimes those three
films you did with someone,

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:58.120
they wanna go and do something
else with somebody else,

00:17:58.120 --> 00:17:59.760
and it's, you just have to learn

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.820
how to navigate that emotionally, but...

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:05.250
- That comes with age, right?

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:08.683
- I think it comes with age, (laughs) yes.

00:18:08.683 --> 00:18:11.640
Yes, yeah, so that's a little tricky too

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.870
because, yeah, you don't,

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:15.770
sometimes you don't do
the five movies together.

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:16.810
Sometimes you do the two,

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:21.580
or sometimes you do the three, but yeah.

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:23.850
- But what I, something
I find when you do,

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:25.630
when you are lucky enough to repeat,

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:26.700
and totally, I agree with you:

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:28.540
you understand they work
with different actors.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:31.530
They work with different
designers and locations.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.080
Why not a different casting director?

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:36.090
But when we are lucky enough to repeat,

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.020
there's such a sense of

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.560
it's such a hard process
falling in love with actors

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:43.370
and making that final decision.

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:44.960
Yes, that's who I'm gonna put in my movie.

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:46.450
I mean, we know they're always,

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.550
that's always the most
vulnerable for the directors

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:50.920
when they finally have to commit.

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:53.003
It's so nice when you have that trust,

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:55.540
as a casting director, with that director,

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:57.180
that, like you said, shorthand

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:00.847
or when you can go,
"Oh, I know your taste.

00:19:00.847 --> 00:19:02.497
"I know what you like.

00:19:02.497 --> 00:19:04.577
"Trust me about this actor

00:19:04.577 --> 00:19:06.497
"more than you may or
may be seeing right now

00:19:06.497 --> 00:19:07.400
"in this audition,"

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:10.610
and I love when that trust is there.

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:12.550
- Yeah, and it's okay to actually,

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:15.477
for them to ask, for them
to say, "Okay, really?

00:19:15.477 --> 00:19:16.310
"Do you..."

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.820
I mean, for them to really
kinda look at you and go,

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.100
believe that they can do it.

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:21.606
They can do it and,

00:19:21.606 --> 00:19:22.960
so that's...

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:25.660
Yeah, they're vulnerable,

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:27.110
yeah, they're-
- Yeah, sure.

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:30.033
- 'Cause they've got
a lot on the line too,

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:32.060
just trying to...

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:33.160
They don't wanna make a mistake,

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:36.330
and casting, I think, is a huge part

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:41.290
of the success of a movie, creatively,

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.650
especially creatively, so yeah.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.517
- And I've also found the
more we do this, right?

00:19:46.517 --> 00:19:51.320
It's always, it's really
one of the first things

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:54.830
that the director is
doing, is committing to,

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:58.100
is actively doing, you know what I mean?

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:01.180
And yet, they're finding their way

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.560
no matter how much personal
prep they might have done,

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.333
so it's a little bit like,

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:09.280
I know they're not
consciously thinking this,

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:10.797
but I always think it's like,

00:20:10.797 --> 00:20:13.817
"Well, if we make a decision
of who we're gonna cast,

00:20:13.817 --> 00:20:15.957
"then we're gonna have to
actually go to the next step,

00:20:15.957 --> 00:20:18.240
"which is start making the movie."

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:19.650
- Exactly, which is scary, yes.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:22.030
- Yeah, totally.
- Yeah.

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:24.670
I actually had a director.

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:26.570
We started casting the movie.

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:27.403
They were scared

00:20:27.403 --> 00:20:29.950
'cause they were jumping into
some controversial territory,

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:32.127
and I'd gone to New York,

00:20:32.127 --> 00:20:33.440
and they were gonna come and meet me

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:34.650
to meet a couple of actors,

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.797
and he called me, says, "I can't.

00:20:37.797 --> 00:20:39.437
"I can't do it. I can't."

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:41.733
He wasn't ready yet,

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:45.110
and I went, "It's okay."

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:47.590
I think I kinda got it.

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:50.410
I knew that there was some...

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:52.070
It wasn't prima donna behavior.

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:54.330
It was just-
- Right.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:57.957
- I said, "That's okay. We'll
just figure this out later.

00:20:57.957 --> 00:21:01.028
"I'll tell these people you'll
meet them at another time,"

00:21:01.028 --> 00:21:01.861
but they were really,

00:21:01.861 --> 00:21:04.980
yeah, in terms of that
vulnerability of a director

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:08.210
making those casting decisions.

00:21:08.210 --> 00:21:09.140
I mean, you're right.

00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:11.040
Now you're committed; you're in the movie.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:12.463
You're making it,

00:21:13.301 --> 00:21:15.878
and that was, yeah, that was interesting.

00:21:15.878 --> 00:21:17.500
That was...

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.687
It was a lesson in empathy.

00:21:19.687 --> 00:21:21.520
I mean, yeah, so yeah.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.920
- Talk a little bit about what
you look for in an audition.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.140
What are the nuances?

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:33.760
Or what makes a great audition for you,

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.140
for when you see an actor?

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:40.450
- Wow. How would you answer
that question, Bernie?

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:41.907
- How would I answer?

00:21:41.907 --> 00:21:43.810
(Vickie laughs)

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:46.000
I always, obviously, we have a sense

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.703
of what we believe the,

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:51.117
what the director is looking for,

00:21:51.117 --> 00:21:52.400
and we know what their vision is,

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:54.100
so we have that sense of

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:57.647
do they fit what it is that
we've been told that we need,

00:21:57.647 --> 00:21:58.570
but I think, for me,

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:01.140
it's someone that just
walks into that room

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:02.540
and makes me feel something

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:04.910
in whatever short time I'm in the room.

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:07.330
I mean, it's, I know
that sounds corny for me,

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:10.217
but it's like, "Oh, did I feel something

00:22:10.217 --> 00:22:13.810
"having heard this 65 times or two times?"

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:15.850
Was there something that made me

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:18.040
'cause I feel like I'm always racing,

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:19.730
like I know what we need.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:21.520
I know what you're not,

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:24.230
but it's like, it feels
like all time stops

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:25.900
for that half a second

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.543
because of something
that the actor was doing,

00:22:30.306 --> 00:22:31.620
and they just moved me,

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:33.523
and that-
- Yeah, I think,

00:22:33.523 --> 00:22:35.930
I think maybe people don't
realize that what we do is

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:38.630
we may hear something 62 times,

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:43.630
or we'll read a scene and
nothing rises above...

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.870
It's just all sort of like
this, and you're right.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:49.663
At that moment, when someone
comes in and gives it,

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:51.880
yeah, it makes you feel something

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:54.420
or actually makes the dialogue work

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:56.320
in a different way than anybody else had.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.580
It's like that is that moment,

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:01.010
that sort of a-ha moment,

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:04.127
or yeah, the moment that makes you go,

00:23:04.127 --> 00:23:06.197
"Oh, shit."
- A-ha.

00:23:06.197 --> 00:23:08.810
- I think this part is
actually gonna work,

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:11.850
and there's somebody who
can actually play this part,

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:14.120
even though I've looked
at 100 other people,

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:15.500
and you've gotten depressed because it

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:17.740
nothing seems to be working,

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:20.040
and not that anybody's
giving a bad reading.

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:21.913
It's not that they're all bad.

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:23.880
- Not at all.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:28.120
- It's just that nothing
is sort of rising above,

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:29.610
and yeah, when someone comes in,

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:32.440
and it's like, "Oh,
shoot, oh, oh, that's it,"

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:33.960
yeah, it's exciting.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:35.050
Very exciting,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:38.160
and I don't think you can kinda
define the nuance, exactly.

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:40.390
- [Bernie] Right, right, absolutely.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:43.950
- Depends on each
particular part, or yeah.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:47.640
- What are some of the places
that you look for new talent?

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:51.460
Or was there a specific role
of a movie you were working on

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:55.880
where you had to go outta
the box to find someone

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:59.570
that would be a fun thing for
the students to hear about?

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.477
Of like that "Detective Thomas,"

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:05.710
where you had to go outside of the norm

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:07.470
to maybe find someone,

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:10.230
or just generally, where do you
like to look for new talent?

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:13.220
- Yeah, just wherever it may be.

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:15.310
I did a movie called "A Family Thing"

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:18.110
years ago with Robert Duvall,

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:21.550
and we were looking

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:23.240
for an older black woman

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:27.210
to play, or like the lead
role, a black matriarch,

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:30.880
and I'd been in Texas years
before working on a film,

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:32.720
and I'd met this woman, Irma P. Hall,

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:34.480
and she just seemed to be great,

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:37.130
and she just was mainly in Texas

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:41.507
and did parts in movies
that were based in Texas

00:24:41.507 --> 00:24:45.100
and hadn't really

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:50.060
done anything big outside of
films that were shooting there,

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:53.720
and we lost an actress that
was gonna play this part,

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:56.420
someone more famous,
appropriate, or whatever,

00:24:56.420 --> 00:24:58.040
and that (indistinct),

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.210
and I just went, "Irma P. Hall."

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:01.800
She's in Dallas, Texas.

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:05.340
She's like doing a play at the whatever,

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:08.610
and it's her.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.590
She came in, and booked it,

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:12.430
and so I guess that's an example, maybe,

00:25:12.430 --> 00:25:16.210
of just not necessarily someone
from outside a box, but-

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:17.593
- Sure, sure, sure.

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:22.040
- Someone who wouldn't have
normally gotten that part.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.520
- Yeah, that's the, right, sure.

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:27.770
- And but in, I mean,
look, I think it, also,

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:29.790
it depends what sort of
movies we're doing, Bernie,

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:33.380
in terms of how much you'll
be able to go outside of a box

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:34.213
in a certain way.

00:25:34.213 --> 00:25:36.920
There's some films that
lend themselves to,

00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:40.963
well, you have to find a short,

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:44.240
a little person,

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:48.418
whatever the specific little thing is.

00:25:48.418 --> 00:25:50.430
- (indistinct) put on the detective hat,

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:51.737
and you start looking.
- Exactly, exactly,

00:25:51.737 --> 00:25:54.830
and it's fun to enter that world.

00:25:54.830 --> 00:25:56.897
I'm looking for a deaf kid right now,

00:25:58.111 --> 00:25:59.400
and it's just like,

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:01.730
it's fun to sort of be
an explorer in that world

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:03.740
and really try to find,

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:07.700
whether it's through deaf
societies or wherever,

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:10.418
find someone who could make that,

00:26:10.418 --> 00:26:11.251
who could do that part,

00:26:11.251 --> 00:26:14.360
so I'll try to figure out a story

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:17.700
of someone outside the
box that I've cast before.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:18.970
- No, that's all right.

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:21.430
Are there any

00:26:22.730 --> 00:26:24.830
well-known actors today,

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:26.290
but when they weren't well-known

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:28.760
or when they were just
starting out in the business,

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:30.680
that they came in and auditioned for you

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:32.350
for a certain project,

00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:35.697
but then you now watched years later,

00:26:35.697 --> 00:26:37.960
"Oh my God, now they're an Oscar nominee."

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:38.830
Are there any sort of-

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:41.820
- Yeah, you know, Don Cheadle,

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:45.530
who came in, I think, like
in '80, in the late '80s,

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:47.740
he played a soda fountain jerk

00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:50.210
in a low-budget movie for me. (laughs)

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:51.120
- Oh my God. I love that.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:52.670
- Yeah, yeah, when he first start,

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:54.590
think he was at Cal Arts,

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:55.423
but he'd just started.

00:26:55.423 --> 00:26:57.833
He did a day. Soda fountain jerk.

00:27:03.137 --> 00:27:06.930
I mean, I just remember Catherine Keener

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:10.223
just auditioning all the time
and never getting the part,

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:13.230
but you'd always bring her,

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:14.450
and years later, she said to me,

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:16.277
said, "Vickie, I just wanna thank you

00:27:16.277 --> 00:27:17.337
"for always bringing me in.

00:27:17.337 --> 00:27:18.170
"I never got anything.

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:20.437
"I just wanna thank you
for bringing me back,"

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.090
but you figure these people,
Bernie, they're good.

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:26.160
You bring 'em back in.

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:27.263
Something's gonna hit,

00:27:29.006 --> 00:27:30.930
but yeah, I think Don
as a soda fountain jerk.

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:35.010
I mean, look, Gary Oldman
was casting "Sid and Nancy."

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:36.160
We had London and U.S. casting.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:38.490
He was casting, he was
casting outta London,

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:40.700
and I remember seeing
him for the first time

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:43.470
arriving at JFK in New York

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:46.190
to start work on "Sid and Nancy,"

00:27:46.190 --> 00:27:47.820
and there's this like this little guy

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:50.010
leaning against the wall,

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:53.730
and the most unassuming guy,

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:56.520
and it was like that's,

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:58.637
I mean, you would never guess
he would be Gary Oldman,

00:27:58.637 --> 00:28:00.680
but he was just very unassuming.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:03.610
His first big role,

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:06.520
and yeah,

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:09.560
he became what he became.

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:12.483
I mean Viggo was, used
to hang out with Viggo.

00:28:14.510 --> 00:28:17.330
Santa Monica, would do like a small part

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.390
in a movie called "Roadside Prophets,"

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:21.910
and it's, I don't know.

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:23.550
You see them sort of take off-

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:24.383
- No, I know.

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:27.240
The Viggo story reminds me of

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:31.250
I was casting a off-Broadway
production for Ivo van Hove,

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:34.090
who's a wonderful European director,

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:36.337
but he was doing his
version of "Streetcar,"

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:39.980
and this was way before
he was doing movies,

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.790
but Viggo came in,

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:43.860
and it was like New York guy,

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:45.470
and it was like, oh my God,

00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:48.690
the most amazing Stanley audition,

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:50.260
and then we offer it to him,

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:52.230
and then, of course, in the next minute,

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:55.460
one of you L.A. casting
directors snapped him up,

00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:56.700
and he became a movie star,

00:28:56.700 --> 00:29:00.150
and I don't think he even, has
never been back to the stage,

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:03.050
but it was like, "Damn!" (laughs)

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:05.480
But it happens. We all watch it happen.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:06.590
It's so great when it happens.

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:10.210
- It really is, and he was
sort the reluctant movie star.

00:29:10.210 --> 00:29:11.043
He was-

00:29:11.043 --> 00:29:11.920
- Yeah, totally.

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:12.786
Probably still is.
- (indistinct) really.

00:29:12.786 --> 00:29:13.619
- I don't know him, but.

00:29:13.619 --> 00:29:14.880
- We had to kinda really talk to him

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.848
to come and read on "Crimson Tide."

00:29:16.848 --> 00:29:17.681
- Wow.

00:29:17.681 --> 00:29:19.681
- Yeah, and he came in 'cause it just...

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:23.293
He marches to his own drumbeat.

00:29:24.641 --> 00:29:28.253
This was sort of a Jerry
Bruckheimer commercial movie,

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:31.077
and you're trying to...

00:29:31.077 --> 00:29:32.250
"No, come on in and do it."

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:33.580
It's like "Con Air."

00:29:33.580 --> 00:29:35.330
John Cusack and Steve Buscemi

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:37.373
were all independent film guys,

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:41.410
and you say, "Oh, well, come
on for this Jerry Bruckheimer."

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:44.540
(laughs) They had a
certain, and I love Jerry.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:45.420
He's a great producer,

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:49.440
but he had a certain sort of
Hollywood commercial sheen

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:54.180
that they were not usually in,

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:58.250
and to watch them sort
of do one of those movies

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:00.850
and actually come out the better for it,

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:04.360
I mean, it helped them continue to do

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:05.790
the independent things they wanted to do

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:09.380
and also do, open up,
maybe, an audience for them,

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:11.167
so I don't know.

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:13.490
- How have you felt in these,

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:15.900
in the last few, I was gonna say months,

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.130
but it's really, I
guess, almost two years,

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:19.520
casting virtually?

00:30:19.520 --> 00:30:21.480
How is that affected?

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:23.800
What are the pros and the cons?

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:26.240
- Well, the pros are you
can see many more people-

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:27.073
- I know.
- in a

00:30:27.073 --> 00:30:30.530
shorter amount of time,
so that's kinda great,

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:31.363
but I-

00:30:31.363 --> 00:30:32.950
- And from anywhere.

00:30:32.950 --> 00:30:36.860
- From anywhere, and you also sort of see

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:39.170
a certain sort of screen

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:45.100
magnitude, yeah, magnitude
is maybe the wrong word,

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:46.140
but does somebody,

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:48.590
is somebody able to hold
the screen a little bit,

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:51.750
but then, look, it's been frustrating

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:52.770
for directors I work with.

00:30:52.770 --> 00:30:54.240
They wanna be in a room with an actor

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:56.530
and see them in the
flesh and work with them,

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:59.580
and so in that sense,
I miss the other thing,

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:01.870
but I don't think it's gonna go back

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:04.020
just because there is a convenience to it.

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:05.183
- That's my fear.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:08.740
I mean, it's gone back in the theater.

00:31:08.740 --> 00:31:10.030
I do some theater, of course,

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:12.173
and that's been in-person,

00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:16.610
but I do feel like the film and TV

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:21.190
was already leaning towards not in-person,

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:26.085
that now the convenience
for some are outweighing.

00:31:26.085 --> 00:31:28.110
- Yeah, yeah.

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:29.837
I miss being in the room with actors-

00:31:29.837 --> 00:31:31.780
- I miss it so much.

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:34.320
- Yeah, I've done a few in-person things.

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:38.710
It's usually such a to-do
with the test and thing

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:39.543
and blah-blah-blah-blah,

00:31:39.543 --> 00:31:42.010
but it's just really good
to be in a room with them,

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:43.960
but I realize it's just not gonna go back

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.750
completely the way it was so.

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:49.320
- We just have to put
singing in every movie

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:50.670
'cause that's been our way.

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:52.570
We're doing the "Wicked" film,

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:53.960
but because we're doing the musicals,

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:56.120
it's like we have to do them in-person.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:57.063
- Ah, that's good.
- Because

00:31:57.063 --> 00:31:58.680
you can't sing virtually,

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:00.225
so it's like-

00:32:00.225 --> 00:32:01.451
- That's right-
- Now i'm telling everyone.

00:32:01.451 --> 00:32:03.240
Put a song. Put in a song.

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:05.373
- Yeah, so most of your theater things,

00:32:06.280 --> 00:32:07.390
you're doing in-person.

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:10.730
- Yeah, I mean, the
prescreens are on virtual-

00:32:10.730 --> 00:32:11.563
- Yeah, yeah.

00:32:11.563 --> 00:32:12.677
- just one of us,

00:32:12.677 --> 00:32:16.490
but the themes, the things with
the teams are all in-person.

00:32:16.490 --> 00:32:19.543
- I remember before we shut down,

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:22.050
a couple weeks before we shut down,

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:22.883
someone was saying to me,

00:32:22.883 --> 00:32:26.320
"Well, so and so does all their prereads

00:32:29.257 --> 00:32:32.967
"with self tapes and
then they pick from those

00:32:32.967 --> 00:32:34.520
"who they want to come
and read in-person."

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:35.510
Went, "Really?"

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:37.260
Went, "Oh, huh, that's kinda good,"

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:38.600
'cause I would do all the prescreens.

00:32:38.600 --> 00:32:39.650
I would...

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:40.870
- Me too-

00:32:40.870 --> 00:32:41.730
- I would do 'em all,

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:43.627
and I was going, but
something in my head went,

00:32:43.627 --> 00:32:45.750
"Oh, that's kinda great," (chuckles)

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:46.670
and little did I know

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:48.930
that that was what was gonna
be sort of forced on us

00:32:48.930 --> 00:32:53.320
in the next few months, so yeah.

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:54.153
- Right.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:57.630
Before we get to some of the questions

00:32:57.630 --> 00:32:59.960
from our FilmAid students in Kenya,

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:01.520
I would love to ask the question of

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:04.427
what would you tell someone who said,

00:33:04.427 --> 00:33:06.490
"I wanna go into casting,"

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:09.120
and they're just getting outta school

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:12.090
and whether there's a job or not,

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:14.853
what would you say to someone
who's interested in casting?

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:18.610
- I think I would sort of encourage it,

00:33:18.610 --> 00:33:21.503
and I would,

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:27.403
I would wanna know why. (laughs)

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:31.981
- Don't ask me why.

00:33:31.981 --> 00:33:32.900
(both laughing)

00:33:32.900 --> 00:33:34.730
- Really? First of all...

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:42.393
No, you know, our job is a
little like psychologist.

00:33:43.266 --> 00:33:44.553
It's recognizing talent.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:48.556
It's a lot of different components,

00:33:48.556 --> 00:33:49.389
and

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:54.380
as long as people are
sort of ready for that,

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:56.520
I sometimes, I'm not sure
if people really know

00:33:56.520 --> 00:33:58.353
what being in casting means.

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:00.183
- Right, sure.

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:05.010
- So I'd probably ask them
what they thought it was,

00:34:05.010 --> 00:34:08.180
what they think they might like about it,

00:34:08.180 --> 00:34:11.713
and hopefully, encourage,

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:13.453
encourage someone

00:34:13.453 --> 00:34:15.953
who really seemed to have
a passion for actors,

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:23.020
yeah, but I think, I don't
think it's a closed-off,

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:26.673
I don't think it's a closed-off job.

00:34:27.996 --> 00:34:31.090
- And I think there's so
much, I'd say a young person,

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:35.180
they can do now, before
even having that job.

00:34:35.180 --> 00:34:36.870
They live in the world of the Internet.

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:37.860
Start taking notes

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:41.470
of every film or TV or
theater thing that they saw,

00:34:41.470 --> 00:34:45.748
and start to hone in on their taste.

00:34:45.748 --> 00:34:47.440
People ask me, "Well, what is taste?"

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:48.927
It's like, "Well, go see something

00:34:48.927 --> 00:34:52.057
"and start talking out
loud or talking to yourself

00:34:52.057 --> 00:34:54.527
"about what you thought
of those performances

00:34:54.527 --> 00:34:56.517
"and who stood out,

00:34:56.517 --> 00:34:59.930
"even if it was the one-line part, and"-

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:01.390
- I often tell...

00:35:01.390 --> 00:35:06.120
I think a sense of film history

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:10.050
and theater history,

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:12.630
just the great performances,
the great actors.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:15.677
I always tell actors, "Just
go look at great actors,

00:35:15.677 --> 00:35:19.862
"whether they're from the '90s, the '80s,

00:35:19.862 --> 00:35:23.420
"the '60s, the '50s, the
'40s are great actors."

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:26.977
I said, "Adopt an actor, and
just look at all their movies,

00:35:26.977 --> 00:35:28.430
"and sorta get to know

00:35:29.457 --> 00:35:33.357
"what great screen acting could be like."

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:36.450
- I love that: adopt an actor.

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:37.497
It's a great one.

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:39.494
It's a great one,

00:35:39.494 --> 00:35:44.283
and I love that suggestion,
too, of watching it in order.

00:35:44.283 --> 00:35:45.420
- Mm-hmm. Yes.

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:46.633
- You know what I mean? And you see.

00:35:46.633 --> 00:35:48.380
I mean, a friend of mine did it.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:49.420
Yes, it sounded like fun.

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:51.010
They watched every Meryl Streep movie,

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:54.270
from the first one to the last,

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:55.490
but they really came at it

00:35:55.490 --> 00:35:57.860
from a sense of watching the growth.

00:35:57.860 --> 00:35:59.690
I mean, yes, you
(indistinct) done day one,

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:03.680
but yes, it's just that's a
really great way to learn.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:05.210
- Exactly.
- By watching how they change,

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:07.499
how they grow.
- Exactly. Exactly.

00:36:07.499 --> 00:36:09.580
Exactly. Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:36:09.580 --> 00:36:11.200
No, I think it's a great profession,

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:12.610
and I feel like our profession

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:15.970
has gotten more visibility,
more recognition,

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.198
and it's a place to make a living.

00:36:18.198 --> 00:36:19.870
That-
- It is,

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:21.547
and I think it's,

00:36:21.547 --> 00:36:25.270
I mean, I really do think it's one of,

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:26.940
I think it's all creative positions

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:30.130
on the making of a film
or a theater piece,

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:34.900
but it's you really can impact something

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:40.513
in a big way,

00:36:41.980 --> 00:36:43.593
and that's nerve-wracking too.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:47.360
I feel the burden in a way,

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:49.520
and I just, "Oh my God,
what are we gonna do?"

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:51.647
But it's you can really have an impact.

00:36:52.669 --> 00:36:54.570
- All right, I'm gonna ask some of the...

00:36:54.570 --> 00:36:55.940
We had some really good questions

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:58.510
from our Kenya FilmAid students

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:00.127
if you have a few extra minutes.

00:37:00.127 --> 00:37:01.560
- [Vickie] Sure.

00:37:01.560 --> 00:37:02.393
- Great.

00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:05.734
How long does it take you to cast

00:37:05.734 --> 00:37:10.293
or get the right actor in
the right role? (laughs)

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:13.380
How much time do you have

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:14.257
is the question, right?
- I (indistinct) worked

00:37:14.257 --> 00:37:16.590
on something for three months,

00:37:16.590 --> 00:37:20.530
and we still hadn't
found the right person,

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:23.740
and that's really sort of
nervewracking, so depends.

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:27.412
Sometimes you get in there
the first couple weeks

00:37:27.412 --> 00:37:31.470
(indistinct) or the, you kinda
think you know who it is,

00:37:31.470 --> 00:37:33.400
and yeah, three weeks later, four weeks,

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:34.825
yeah, that's who it was,

00:37:34.825 --> 00:37:38.060
but I literally worked on some

00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:39.990
that were three months,

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:42.160
and, I mean, actually,
it was longer than that.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:43.030
We didn't have anybody.

00:37:43.030 --> 00:37:44.880
It was very nervewracking,

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:48.040
and so it just depends.

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:48.873
Just depends.

00:37:48.873 --> 00:37:52.830
- Right, right, I always feel
like there's one role that's-

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:54.374
- The bane of your existence?
- impossible to cast.

00:37:54.374 --> 00:37:55.780
- Yeah, the band of your existence.

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:57.480
- Yeah, there's just always one role.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:37:59.413
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:38:01.270 --> 00:38:04.170
- In your experience, obviously,

00:38:04.170 --> 00:38:06.080
maybe they don't know this
or do know this, students,

00:38:06.080 --> 00:38:08.538
we do have casting contracts.

00:38:08.538 --> 00:38:09.730
Is there anything

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:13.370
that you feel like you
would wanna add to...

00:38:13.370 --> 00:38:15.610
Is there something that is not there now

00:38:15.610 --> 00:38:19.790
that you would want there
in a casting contract?

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:22.273
- Residuals. (laughs)

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:25.740
- I like it. I like it.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:28.830
Let's call 399 and 817.

00:38:28.830 --> 00:38:31.810
- I mean, I cast the original "Power,"

00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:35.413
and that's gone off into like
eight spinoffs or whatever,

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:39.020
and I would love to have like had a...

00:38:39.020 --> 00:38:42.830
Anyway, shared in that success

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:45.400
in a longer way,

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:47.060
but let's not talk about all that.

00:38:47.060 --> 00:38:48.470
In a contract...

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:53.550
Wow.

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:55.446
- Yeah, but that's a good one.

00:38:55.446 --> 00:38:59.150
That's a (laughs) a good one,

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:00.790
or bonuses.

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:04.540
- Something. Something
where you share in the...

00:39:06.920 --> 00:39:07.770
- [Bernie] Right.

00:39:09.103 --> 00:39:09.936
- The success.

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.110
- One of the students' questions was

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:18.163
if you're trying to land
a star for a lead role,

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:20.370
what is your approach?

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:22.310
How do you approach that, specifically,

00:39:22.310 --> 00:39:24.930
differently than just casting a movie,

00:39:24.930 --> 00:39:27.510
when you know you have to land a star?

00:39:27.510 --> 00:39:28.760
How do you go about that?

00:39:30.109 --> 00:39:32.400
- Well, hopefully, you have
a director and a script

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:35.523
that will appeal

00:39:39.520 --> 00:39:41.063
to that star.

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:45.963
Wow, it's (indistinct) a star.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:48.490
Through any means necessary.

00:39:48.490 --> 00:39:52.460
I mean, that's really,
it's like call the manager.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:55.190
Okay, let me call him and
tell him this is coming,

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:57.680
just any way you can get something

00:39:57.680 --> 00:40:01.980
to make it rise above
the rest of the script

00:40:03.849 --> 00:40:05.023
someone's being sent.

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:09.360
Yeah, that's a hard one to sort of...

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:11.220
Maybe the approach is

00:40:14.293 --> 00:40:15.570
a little more delicate.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:18.610
- Yeah 'cause we, all of a sudden,

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:20.460
depending on the level of
the director and the script,

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:22.045
we become salespeople.

00:40:22.045 --> 00:40:23.050
- Yes.

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:24.493
- And we're trying to,

00:40:26.710 --> 00:40:28.130
I would say, first off,

00:40:28.130 --> 00:40:30.630
try to get the agent or
manager to read the script

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:33.790
in hopes of having a conversation

00:40:33.790 --> 00:40:37.120
about that actor for this director,

00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:38.430
yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:39.930
No, we are the sales people, Bernie.

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:43.833
It's, yeah, and that's, yeah. Yeah?

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:49.270
- When you have to work on a project

00:40:49.270 --> 00:40:53.150
where you know you're gonna
have to look for people

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:57.440
who have, who don't have a
lot of experience in acting,

00:40:57.440 --> 00:40:59.520
like a lot of young
people just starting out,

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:03.410
is there something you
look for differently?

00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:07.510
How do you handle knowing that
you're not gonna have a cast

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:09.710
with a lot of experience?

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:10.793
And is that okay?

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:13.790
One of them wanted to know.

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:16.250
- Yeah, I think, hopefully,

00:41:16.250 --> 00:41:19.930
you look for someone, even if
they're not that experienced,

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:21.410
who seems to

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:26.800
get better with each audition.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:28.830
They seem to have a natural

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:32.040
instinct for it.

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:33.453
They seem to,

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:37.651
outside of what you're telling them to do,

00:41:37.651 --> 00:41:41.940
they're making decisions about
what to do as that character

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:43.780
that makes you kinda sit up.

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:45.830
It's like, "Oh, okay, they're thinking."

00:41:45.830 --> 00:41:48.580
They're not just repeating

00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:52.880
just repeating lines or
repeating going down a,

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:56.260
down an avenue that I may suggest to them.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:59.180
They're actually taking their own accord,

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:02.613
and oh, they're coming up
with something on their own,

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:04.873
so that's, (indistinct),

00:42:04.873 --> 00:42:07.187
"Oh, okay, well, that's interesting,"

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:11.130
and hopefully, I think,

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:12.940
maybe what we try to do
with inexperienced people

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:14.070
is maybe surround them with someone

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:15.780
who's a little more experienced.

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:17.333
If you can put someone who's,

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:20.060
maybe you have an actor who
knows a little bit more,

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:21.067
been around a little bit more,

00:42:21.067 --> 00:42:22.090
and they can be around

00:42:22.090 --> 00:42:24.310
that person's with a
little less experience,

00:42:24.310 --> 00:42:29.310
it's just they can kind of,
it's a good influence on them,

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:31.540
so I wonder, was this question

00:42:31.540 --> 00:42:34.340
someone who was trying to
figure out how to make a movie

00:42:37.662 --> 00:42:40.070
knowing that they're gonna
be using inexperienced actors

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:41.987
and how do you do that?

00:42:44.900 --> 00:42:46.150
- I think it was probably,

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:48.700
it might have been from
one of the acting students.

00:42:48.700 --> 00:42:52.010
What's it like auditioning
actors who lack experience

00:42:52.010 --> 00:42:55.480
or how do you help that?

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:56.623
I think it was maybe,

00:42:57.720 --> 00:42:59.218
but I'm not sure if it
was an acting student

00:42:59.218 --> 00:43:01.135
or a directing student.

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:04.080
- I'm pretty sensitive to that in a room.

00:43:04.080 --> 00:43:05.680
I know there are people who inexperience,

00:43:05.680 --> 00:43:08.152
and they don't quite know
where to sit or what,

00:43:08.152 --> 00:43:11.950
or how to do it, or what
you just try to help them

00:43:11.950 --> 00:43:14.853
in just the act of having to audition,

00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:19.560
which is, yeah, that's a learning curve.

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:20.770
- Yeah, and it's reminding them

00:43:20.770 --> 00:43:24.500
that so much of acting is listening.

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:25.333
- That's a big one.
- They get in the room

00:43:25.333 --> 00:43:27.380
and they start doing,

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:30.870
and so much of film acting
is listening, right?

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:33.773
The camera's on them, and
they're not the ones speaking,

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:39.500
and it's trying to help
them be grounded enough,

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:41.370
to be still enough to just listen

00:43:41.370 --> 00:43:43.720
to what is being said and...

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:45.920
- Well, I think something that
you said too, Bernie, before

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:50.320
is trying to be just like
a kind person in the room

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:51.983
so they're not facing,

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:55.100
just so that they feel

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.390
like they have a little
bit of support in a room,

00:43:57.390 --> 00:43:59.640
you're just trying to be there.

00:43:59.640 --> 00:44:00.720
It doesn't do us any good

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:05.610
to not make the actors comfortable.

00:44:05.610 --> 00:44:07.100
- Yeah, absolutely, I mean.

00:44:07.100 --> 00:44:09.290
- So, you know?

00:44:09.290 --> 00:44:12.290
- As much as I love what we
do, and I know you do too,

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:14.530
it still always surprises and amazes me

00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:17.893
that how this is how people get jobs,

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:20.930
through the action of auditioning

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:22.840
'cause it's so filled

00:44:22.840 --> 00:44:26.463
with so much tension, hope, fear.

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:32.610
Whatever we can do to help
relax it, be in that moment.

00:44:32.610 --> 00:44:35.110
- And I think the repetitive
nature of it, I think,

00:44:35.110 --> 00:44:36.870
doing it over and over
and over and over again

00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:38.470
is gonna help inexperienced actors,

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:42.100
and it's just something you
have to do again and again.

00:44:42.100 --> 00:44:46.370
- Yeah, one more question,
I think, from them is

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:50.010
what is it like working
with a first-time director?

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:55.650
- You know, it depends who
the first-time director

00:44:58.980 --> 00:44:59.973
is, obviously.

00:45:02.508 --> 00:45:03.341
Huh-

00:45:03.341 --> 00:45:04.510
- Let's say it's a director
right outta school.

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:05.343
You know what I mean?

00:45:05.343 --> 00:45:07.240
Or their first feature,

00:45:07.240 --> 00:45:10.320
and you love the script, and
you've decided to cast it.

00:45:10.320 --> 00:45:13.870
- Yeah, I got, I think
you have to be there

00:45:13.870 --> 00:45:16.520
sort of teaching a certain...

00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:19.360
Boy, that's, yeah, that's a whole other,

00:45:19.360 --> 00:45:20.650
that's a whole other thing.

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:23.870
It's...
(Bernie laughs)

00:45:23.870 --> 00:45:26.030
You're there to guide them,

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:29.773
just to guide them, and hopefully, say,

00:45:32.260 --> 00:45:33.660
you're just there to guide them.

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:36.783
It's just a little bit more of a hands-on.

00:45:38.290 --> 00:45:40.223
There is not necessarily a shorthand,

00:45:41.540 --> 00:45:42.630
and they may not necessarily

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:44.230
have gone through a casting process,

00:45:44.230 --> 00:45:46.357
so you really sorta have to say,

00:45:46.357 --> 00:45:48.327
"No, you actually can talk to the actor.

00:45:48.327 --> 00:45:51.110
"You can actually ask
them to do it again,"

00:45:51.110 --> 00:45:52.050
so it's really guiding them

00:45:52.050 --> 00:45:53.480
through the whole process

00:45:56.143 --> 00:45:57.000
of casting,

00:45:57.000 --> 00:46:00.230
and some will have more of a natural...

00:46:00.230 --> 00:46:01.563
It's like a duck to water.

00:46:02.906 --> 00:46:03.856
They'll get into it

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:08.760
and be better at it sooner than others,

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:13.000
and yeah, it's just you're
their guide and teacher and...

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:17.220
- That reminds me, did you have a mentor

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:20.340
or anyone that guided
you through your career,

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:22.289
or at one time in your career?

00:46:22.289 --> 00:46:25.420
- No, I've learned a lot from
people who've worked with me.

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:27.070
I've learned a lot from Jeanne McCarthy.

00:46:27.070 --> 00:46:28.530
I've learned a lot from

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:32.463
directors I've worked with,

00:46:34.350 --> 00:46:39.350
from Frears, just watching them,

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:41.530
watching him, Stephen Frears,

00:46:41.530 --> 00:46:43.010
just see people and make decisions,

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:44.703
and just, (indistinct) I've learned a lot.

00:46:44.703 --> 00:46:45.620
It's interesting

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:49.850
how directors come to
who they want for a role.

00:46:49.850 --> 00:46:51.960
I've tried to pay attention to that.

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:54.403
Quentin, I mean, just looking at him.

00:46:55.990 --> 00:46:58.103
Frears is interesting 'cause he's just,

00:46:59.170 --> 00:47:01.480
it's amorphous, sort of.

00:47:01.480 --> 00:47:04.212
I really admire him as a director

00:47:04.212 --> 00:47:06.170
and as a director of actors,

00:47:06.170 --> 00:47:09.940
and it's, I don't quite know

00:47:09.940 --> 00:47:12.910
how he does it, what he...

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:16.260
I just try to listen to
how he makes decisions.

00:47:16.260 --> 00:47:17.438
Then, later, I'll ask him,

00:47:17.438 --> 00:47:20.859
(Vickie mumbles humorously)
(Bernie laughs)

00:47:20.859 --> 00:47:22.720
Went, "Oh, okay, okay."

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:24.940
It's just interesting, coming, someone,

00:47:24.940 --> 00:47:27.430
some come from like a feeling, from a...

00:47:29.070 --> 00:47:30.142
It's just...

00:47:30.142 --> 00:47:32.200
I've learned a lot from the
directors I've worked with.

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:33.970
- Yeah, sure.

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:34.803
Do you have a,

00:47:38.199 --> 00:47:39.240
I won't call it a favorite,

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:41.660
but is there a movie in your repertoire

00:47:41.660 --> 00:47:43.170
that you've worked on

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:47.010
that was just the experience
of casting that movie

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:51.080
was special for such-and-such reason,

00:47:51.080 --> 00:47:52.860
whether it was an actor,
director, you know what I mean?

00:47:52.860 --> 00:47:54.180
It's not about favorites, like-

00:47:54.180 --> 00:47:55.013
- [Vickie] Yeah.

00:47:55.013 --> 00:47:55.920
- Can you give a little,

00:47:55.920 --> 00:47:57.410
tell us about a specific story

00:47:57.410 --> 00:48:01.410
of a film that just, to this
day, you still remember love.

00:48:01.410 --> 00:48:04.130
- I think "Ed Wood" was a really,

00:48:04.130 --> 00:48:07.374
just great from start to finish.

00:48:07.374 --> 00:48:08.830
- [Bernie] (indistinct) love that movie.

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:11.620
- Was working with Tim Burton, who's...

00:48:12.557 --> 00:48:15.009
- Oh my God, I forgot you did that movie.

00:48:15.009 --> 00:48:17.190
That's so good.
(Vickie laughing)

00:48:17.190 --> 00:48:20.520
- It was about sort of
L.A. It was set in L.A.

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:25.513
It was just, as a production,
just a pleasure to work on,

00:48:26.570 --> 00:48:28.210
great people who...

00:48:28.210 --> 00:48:31.670
Sarah Jessica hadn't
done a million things,

00:48:31.670 --> 00:48:36.040
and all these people that were
in it playing small parts,

00:48:36.040 --> 00:48:38.000
and it was just a really great...

00:48:39.490 --> 00:48:40.540
That was a really fun experience,

00:48:40.540 --> 00:48:42.300
and "Once Upon a Time in Hollywood."

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:45.670
That felt very personal, very personal

00:48:45.670 --> 00:48:47.240
'cause I'm from L.A.
(Bernie speaks indistinctly)

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:49.300
- All those small parts
in "Hollywood" were

00:48:49.300 --> 00:48:52.276
so amazing.
- Thank you.

00:48:52.276 --> 00:48:54.447
- It was like you just kept wanting to go,

00:48:54.447 --> 00:48:55.280
"Oh my God, who is that?

00:48:55.280 --> 00:48:56.383
"Oh my God, that was her?"

00:48:56.383 --> 00:48:57.216
(Vickie laughing)
You know what I mean,

00:48:57.216 --> 00:48:59.210
but I remember watching that.

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:02.100
It was like, oh, it's so good.

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.438
- And just that it was set in,

00:49:03.438 --> 00:49:06.125
you know, I would go see movies
up on Hollywood Boulevard

00:49:06.125 --> 00:49:07.048
when I was growing up.

00:49:07.048 --> 00:49:07.881
I mean, it was just,

00:49:07.881 --> 00:49:09.600
and I 'member reading about those murders

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:11.777
and my kitchen table
with my mom and my dad.

00:49:11.777 --> 00:49:16.020
It was just, and the cultural
references within the movie,

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:17.580
the TV shows within it, so anyway,

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:20.580
so that was also something
sort of personal,

00:49:20.580 --> 00:49:22.310
even though I didn't know those people.

00:49:22.310 --> 00:49:23.143
I didn't...

00:49:24.670 --> 00:49:26.353
That was great. That was great.

00:49:27.320 --> 00:49:30.373
- Wow, all right, I have
a last question for you.

00:49:35.140 --> 00:49:36.730
If you were starting all over again

00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:38.150
casting from like the day one,

00:49:38.150 --> 00:49:42.910
is there something you wish
you knew that you don't know

00:49:42.910 --> 00:49:44.560
or that you would do differently?

00:49:45.937 --> 00:49:50.094
- I wish I knew that I
was pretty good at it

00:49:50.094 --> 00:49:50.980
(Bernie laughs)

00:49:50.980 --> 00:49:53.810
at an earlier time

00:49:53.810 --> 00:49:57.240
and not been insecure

00:49:58.530 --> 00:50:00.030
because the way I started,

00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:03.120
I was actually hiring people to assist me

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:05.170
who had more experience than I did,

00:50:06.130 --> 00:50:08.293
and I felt a little guilty about that,

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:13.700
and I wish I'd just

00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:15.780
not felt that insecurity

00:50:15.780 --> 00:50:19.680
and trusted that I was pretty good at it,

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:24.520
and don't beat yourself up.

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:27.793
- Right. That's beautiful, actually.

00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:32.460
I still feel like there's
so much we can learn.

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.020
I mean, that's what I
love about the job too

00:50:34.020 --> 00:50:35.550
'cause we're always

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:36.820
learning-
- Yeah, yeah.

00:50:36.820 --> 00:50:39.471
- and if it's just learning a new actor,

00:50:39.471 --> 00:50:43.300
it reminds me that, "Oh, yeah,
I do have to keep learning."

00:50:43.300 --> 00:50:44.760
- Yeah, I don't feel like I know it all.

00:50:44.760 --> 00:50:47.940
I don't feel that way at all,

00:50:47.940 --> 00:50:51.020
and I feel like I have to be reminded to,

00:50:51.020 --> 00:50:53.563
I have to just remind myself that,

00:50:55.760 --> 00:50:57.510
yeah, there's always
something new to learn,

00:50:57.510 --> 00:50:59.720
and yeah, yeah.

00:50:59.720 --> 00:51:03.270
- And any final advice
you'd give to our students

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:05.490
who wanna go into, who
wanna be a casting director

00:51:05.490 --> 00:51:07.413
or wanna be an actor or a director?

00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:12.653
- I'd say just have
faith, know your craft.

00:51:14.794 --> 00:51:15.710
- [Bernie] That's a good one.

00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:18.980
- Always, just always feed yourself.

00:51:18.980 --> 00:51:20.123
Just feed yourself,

00:51:23.883 --> 00:51:28.271
like I said, with film history,
but also, just live life,

00:51:28.271 --> 00:51:31.860
and observe life,

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:34.850
and just travel outside

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:37.600
of your little circle,

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:42.573
and experience the world
as much as you can,

00:51:43.870 --> 00:51:46.350
and use that experience

00:51:47.390 --> 00:51:50.463
in whatever craft you choose.

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:54.600
- That's beautiful. Thank you, Vickie.

00:51:54.600 --> 00:51:57.890
Oh my God, it was so great
to get to hear your story

00:51:57.890 --> 00:51:59.790
and how you started in this business,

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:01.580
and this was so nice.

00:52:01.580 --> 00:52:04.200
I feel like I know you
a different way now,

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:05.490
so thank you.
- I hope it was,

00:52:05.490 --> 00:52:08.810
I hope it was helpful or
enlightening in some sort of way.

00:52:08.810 --> 00:52:10.390
- And I wanna thank all of our students

00:52:10.390 --> 00:52:13.060
for participating in the Academy
Visiting Arts Film Series,

00:52:13.060 --> 00:52:15.483
FilmAid edition for casting directors.

